    t:'Virtual School in Iraq: An Oil Province, an Academic City and a Mountain Governorate',
    date:'August 2026 · 8 min read',
    author:'Brendaliz Chelangat', role:'Head of Academics',
    metaTitle:'Virtual School Iraq 2026: Basra, Sulaymaniyah & Duhok — Smartious',
    metaDesc:'Iraq\u2019s deepest international school provision is in Erbil. The oil south, the academic city and the northern governorate each need something it cannot give them.',
    intro:'Erbil holds the deepest international school provision in Iraq, and three of the country\u2019s most distinctive communities are somewhere else. Basra runs one of the world\u2019s significant oil provinces with an internationally recruited technical workforce. Sulaymaniyah is an academic city with English-medium higher education and thinner school provision. Duhok governorate spreads agriculture and trade across mountains and valleys with almost nothing. This article is about all three.',
    sections:[
      {h:'Basra, and a profession that moves',p:'The south holds the major fields that make Iraq one of the world\u2019s significant producers, the ports at Umm Qasr and along the Shatt al-Arab, and petrochemicals and heavy industry. The technical workforce is recruited internationally and moves between operations and countries — the same profile we already teach in Stavanger, Baku, Hassi Messaoud, Macáe, Neuquén, Cabinda and Takoradi. The subject spine is unambiguous: Cambridge A-Level Physics, Chemistry and Mathematics, with Geography as a fourth for geoscience routes. What these families most need beyond the teaching is portability, because a child who changes school system with each posting pays in alignment and sometimes a repeated year.'},
      {h:'Sulaymaniyah, and a city already studying in English',p:'The Kurdistan Region\u2019s cultural and academic centre is home to the American University of Iraq Sulaimani, an English-medium institution with a strong regional reputation, alongside a long university and publishing tradition. School-level international provision is thinner than in Erbil, three hours west. The argument here is the one we make across Iraq but sharpest in this city: a student heading for English-medium higher education benefits from an English-medium externally examined record before they get there, not only a language test score at the point of application.'},
      {h:'Duhok and the northern districts',p:'The northern governorate carries agriculture, the trade corridor running toward Türkiye and its own university, with communities spread across mountains and valleys over a wide area and almost no international provision. Erbil is a couple of hours south-east. For families in Zakho, Amedi or the mountain districts, the constraint is simply distance — and live delivery closes it without a move, with Biology and Chemistry alongside Geography serving the agronomy and environmental routes these households most often have in view.'},
      {h:'What the three share',p:'Distance from provision, and in Basra\u2019s case a household that may move again. That is the shape live delivery answers directly: identical teaching wherever the family is, subject sets assembled from a group drawn across countries, and one examination board that continues to the next posting. Every class is also recorded, so a session that cannot be attended is never a session lost — which matters more in some households than others and costs nothing to provide.'},
      {h:'Planning it, including the two authorities',p:'Two to four Cambridge subjects alongside an Iraqi school enrolment is realistic, concentrated on mathematics, the sciences and English at IGCSE, then two or three A-Levels chosen strictly for the target course, with Arabic or Kurdish home language support kept alongside. And confirm your position with the right ministry: the Kurdistan Region has its own Ministry of Education for Erbil, Sulaymaniyah, Duhok and Halabja, while the federal Ministry of Education administers Basra and the federal governorates. We could not verify a home-education position in either track and say so rather than guessing.'},
    ],
    faqs:[
      {q:'We are on an energy posting in Basra — does the schooling follow?',a:'Yes: the same curriculum, teachers and examination board continue to the next basin or country, with examinations sat at authorised centres wherever the family is.'},
      {q:'Is there international schooling in Duhok or Sulaymaniyah?',a:'Minimal in Duhok and thinner in Sulaymaniyah than in Erbil. Live delivery reaches both identically, with examination arrangements planned well ahead.'},
      {q:'Our child wants petroleum engineering — what should they take?',a:'Cambridge A-Level Physics, Chemistry and Mathematics, with Geography as a fourth for geoscience routes.'},
      {q:'Which ministry applies to us?',a:'The Kurdistan Region\u2019s own for Erbil, Sulaymaniyah, Duhok and Halabja; the federal Ministry of Education elsewhere, including Basra and Baghdad.'},
    ],
    conclusion:'An oil province, an academic city and a mountain governorate — three distinct gaps, one pathway, and no clock to work around. Book an assessment for your child, or start with our Basra, Sulaymaniyah and Duhok pages.',
